  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 338 PM EDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nThe stretch of unsettled weather over the region begins Friday night \nas a nearly vertically stacked upper-level low moves in from the \nwest, with the low closing off in the process. Chances for showers \nand thunderstorms will increase as the night progresses as a 20-30 \nkt SW LLJ out ahead of the low will advect in a warm and moist \nairmass. PW values will quickly increase, with the latest LREF \ndepicting a transition from 1.2-1.4\" to 1.9-2.1\" by Saturday \nmorning. While some heavier downpours will be possible with this \nfirst wave of showers and storms late Friday night/Saturday morning, \nthe threat for severe weather is very low. Overnight temperatures \nwill be milder, dropping into the upper 60s/low 70s. \n\nNumerous showers and scattered thunderstorms are expected Saturday \nahead of the low's cold front moving through Saturday evening/night. \nRecent guidance has been showing a developing band of showers and \nstorms along the cold front Saturday afternoon/evening, though there \nis still some uncertainty regarding the intensity of these storms. \nOver the past few model runs, there have been disagreements on a few \nfactors, including: placement/timing of the surface low and cold \nfront, how long the morning convection lasts over the area, and how \nmuch destabilization occurs before the second wave in the \nafternoon. The modest 25-30 kt SW LLJ will continue to pump in warm \nand moist air, with dew points rising into the low 70s. Places that \nget less rainfall from the Friday night/Saturday morning wave could \ndestabilize more, with the most recent LREF outputting >1500 J/kg of \nMUCAPE in parts of central Kentucky. \n\nThough modest low-level wind shear and backed SSE surface winds \nahead of the surface low do result in enhanced low-level curvature \non model hodographs, weaker deep-layer shear would be another factor \nthat could limit the potential for strong-to-severe thunderstorms. \nThe SPC currently has most of the area under a Marginal Risk for \nsevere weather, with the main threat being damaging winds as well as \na low-end threat for a tornado or two. This continues to be a \nconditional risk for severe weather late Saturday, with confidence \nstill remaining fairly low. With the elevated (1.9-2.1\" PW) amount \nof moisture over the area thanks to the persistent LLJ, a localized \nflooding threat is possible from the rainfall on Saturday. The WPC \nhas us under a Slight Risk for excessive rainfall. Ensemble \nprecipitation totals have trended slightly down in recent runs, \nthough widespread amounts of 1-2\" with locally higher amounts still \npossible. \n\nFollowing the passage of the cold front Saturday evening/night, the \nvertically stacked low will remain situated over the Ohio Valley \nthrough midweek, resulting in chances for diurnally driven showers \nand storms during the afternoon hours Sunday through Wednesday. The \nlow will finally push off to the east on Thursday, with lower \nchances for showers and storms as well that day. Maximum \ntemperatures Sunday-Wednesday will be in the low to mid 80s while \nthe low remains overhead, climbing into the upper 80s once the low \nbegins to depart.",
